n is counting down his time  in the parliament  although he could see this day coming it  still came as a shock  i never thought it would be so fast so  hard  we often say xi jinping is the new  emperor  more ruthless and domineering than many  leaders in the past  probably as bird as mao zedong now we  see  that he could even end one country two  systems  in a blink of an eye after starting out  as a social worker and university  lecturer  she became one of the leading activists  in the umbrella movement  in 2014 and was sentenced to eight  months in jail last year  as a legislator it was a traumatic  experience for him  but he still keeps his belongings from  prison to remind himself of his mission  in the past four years becoming a  lawmaker was a peak of my life  but then i was sent to prison the lowest  point of my life  it was so tough in there and i had to  find meaning of  life by fighting for prisoners rights i  taught myself  i'm an undercover lawmaker in jail i'm  here as a prisoner for the checks on  possible abuse by prison officers  i'm here to monitor the government  since the anti-government movement  started in hong kong last year  over 10 000 people have been arrested  with the rise of a generation of  political prisoners  she will seize the pressing need to  provide support for these young people  and their families  he initiated a pan-friend scheme earlier  this year  and has handled 6 000 letters so far  he plans to organize pre-jail workshops  after he leaves the council  being in prison is losing freedom by  having a lot of time  it is the same with leaving the  legislative council  i will use public resources but in  return have more time to focus on this  particular  issue i don't know how far i can go with  the fight on prisoners rights  but i promise i will go as far as i can  but what does the resignation of  lawmakers like shukachun  mean for ordinary hong kongers it's a  question i'm going to put to 27 year old  connor  who joins me on 