trictions now than they used to  but it's still really pivotal to  the movement in general i think a major  shift  going forward would be a shift towards  the world stage and the international  stage  because obviously with um a lot  of the movements leaders being exiled  most of the proponents have moved  to towards the uh international  community but then  going by your argument that would  suggest that uh within hong kong itself  there aren't any avenues left to protest  or to stand up for  democratic rights hence it would appear  that the democratic movement in hong  kong itself is dead  right um so yeah i don't think the  movement's dead it's just been  that the course has been changed and a  lot of the methods needs to be changed  as well along with changing times  yeah so it's not dead it's just  functioning in some other ways  i think domestic resistance what is the  kind of domestic resistance you're  talking about  right so i've seen a lot of  things being proposed by leaders of the  movement  so um one uh two things that i've seen  in the past couple of months  it's um a push by a lot of grass move  grassroots forces towards  the rebuilding of a hong kong economy a  more diverse hong kong  economy with the um central planning all  and all that  hong kong for the past 20 years after  the handover  in that time frame we have lost a lot of  our domestic produce and a domestic  industry  uh we we were we used to be in the face  innovative proponents in um  tech and manufacturing um but that's  been dead  for quite some time and we are all  putting everything into travel and  financials  so i think uh seeing a grassroots  push towards the revival of a hong kong  culture  and hong kong community that's a way of  resistance yeah  so yes that's the main thing  we leave it there for the time being but  thank you so much for speaking to us  connor speaking to us from hong kong  thank you  you
 

